  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nActive weather will persist through today and past mid week. A \nfrontal boundary will drape across the region and will meander \nthrough Monday while pushing slowly south and finally exiting \nby mid day Monday. This feature will provide chances for shower\nand storm activity and where a few storms could be strong to \nsevere which should be isolated in nature. The main threat will\nbe damaging wind. Much of the activity will be confined to the \nnorthern parts of our Country Warning Area where higher \nprobabilities of precipitation exist. \n\nAnother aspect is the area will be under an excessive rainfall \noutlook and the threat of heavy downpours amid thunderstorm \nactivity will likely cause some instances of flash flooding to \nflood prone or low lying areas if hit repeatedly. This will be\nsupported by anomalously high PWATs for this time of year. Once\nthe feature exits a low pressure system digging and \nstrengthening across the Great Lakes will come into the region \nand promote synoptic scale ascent to broaden the unsettled \nweather across our entire area.\n\nFor much of Monday and Tuesday and even Wednesday will be under\nthe influence of broad low pressure and chances for shower and \nthunderstorms. This is especially for Tuesday and Wednesday \nwith high probabilities of precipitation especially during \nthe daytime. This activity along with weak cold air advection \nwill promote temperatures below seasonable starting Tuesday and \ncontinuing into the weekend promoting comfortable weather amid \ndrier conditions as strong high pressure builds in for the the \nend of the work week and for the weekend.",
